es the ashley era has been  devoid of hope  um it has been tergen football  it has been um to quote steve bruce our  current head coach ticking along you  know that the mike ashley era to me as a  fan  has been about remaining in the premier  league but doing so  at sort of at baseline cost we have been  relegated twice since mike ashley has  run the club um we've come back uh each  time because of uh  investment he's put in but immediately  then taken out  um we don't  buy any uh  exciting players or we do so very  infrequently um and and it's about  sticking plasters over quite significant  wounds for me uh that's what that's what  his tenure has been like  so newcastle united fans want change  they don't want mike ashley  are they happy with having saudi arabia  instead of mike ashley because you know  the question i'm going to ask charlotte  this is going to be a controversial deal  if it does go through human rights  groups have already pointed out and you  know people are well aware of saudi  arabia's  human rights record  it is not good a newcastle united fans  happy to be associated with that or will  they just as my colleague jane zecker  put it earlier on to another fans group  just hold their nose and get on with it  i think it's a really big question and i  can't answer on behalf of every  newcastle united fan um if they're happy  to be owned by  the public investment fund of saudi  arabia should that come to fruition  today  i think um  i think the happiness on tyneside right  now is that mike ashley is gone or  we cross our fingers that that is the  case  um  i i i think it's really difficult i  think  i think that ambition has returned to  the club and and  if you look at twitter newcastle united  twitter has been so divided over the  last year and a half two years  to see it just united in excitement  about what the future might hold i i  think that's what that's what fans are  focusing on right now  and charlotte i will point out as many  people on 